stion-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/b23ef6e4df05874a715271c578dfa57d5ddacabef2d0801edc4e736c7de66e17.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122319" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94"} {"question" "q-122319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122319" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/473a3f91d91351499d5849ee07a86cfcfc2b257949d6ebba68b3261201f2bf68.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94"} {"question" "q-122307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}"] ["math" "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}"] ["math" "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}"] ["math" "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $-4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/466c0aa389beb6d1a42092b73bae75508ef2c50f1f08cca5efaac20d25615f30.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4(1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4(x+{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-4)^{2} & =4(1+{1}^{2})+C \\\\ 8 & =8+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8(x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=-(2+x+{x}^{2})"] ["math" "v(x)=-2(x+{x}^{2})"] ["math" "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}"] ["math" "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{3x+{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "v(x)=-\\sqrt{2(3x+5{x}^{2})}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122340" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $3\\,m$ when its velocity is $2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/164c90893a07ae9657e90b6f2b25e53c9c4927b1f4a076429ba0521a98377f24.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={e}^{x-3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=3\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & ={e}^{3-3}+C \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+1 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({e}^{x-3}+1) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=3{e}^{x-3}-1"] ["math" "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\sqrt{3{e}^{x-3}+1}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x-3}+3}"] ["math" "v(x)={e}^{x-3}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/4e0b8b816ca3b86fa90ea785df1308f53a4763baa2c2aa2192b9d7bfa0259855.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & ={1}^{4}+2(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 3 & =3+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({2}^{4}+2(2)^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "4\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "5\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+x+2}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+6}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}"] ["math" "v(x)={x}^{2}+3x"] ["math" "v(x)={x}^{2}+x+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $0\\,m$ when its velocity is $-2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/ba6a85da04d9ba03bcdba9b938dac208a770933310cd25c19bf0ec3e19ef17c7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (\\frac{1}{16}x)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{2}+64) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+16}"] ["math" "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}"] ["math" "v(x)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "v(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{64-{x}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122339" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=(12t-1)^{3}+1"] ["math" "x(t)=(3t+2)^{3}-8"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{2t+1}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t-8}+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+4{x}^{2})\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{8}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/59c4a51d419b6e42633579b687cde16a2842895c589f818942c10aa304bc98a7.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2x & =t+K \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=0,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7aab2d70df29993bcf3933ea0964c05d6c71bd11eb5e3841881fcf936561eec"} {"question" "q-122312" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "785f7610edd208db81cfb7c4ec13a140b5ce47e04ba0e053de3c67f7552cdd94"} {"questi